WEBVTT
TITLE Regular Expressions And Finite Automata — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
A language is just a set of strings. A machine is a yes-or-no answer for every string.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Five parts make a deterministic machine: states, alphabet, transition rule, start state, accepting states.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:24.000
The state is the only memory the machine has. After a prefix it knows nothing else.

4
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:32.000
So every question in this topic reduces to one: how much must the machine remember, and in how few states?

5
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Delta takes a state and one symbol and gives exactly one state. Single-valued is what deterministic means.

6
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:48.000
The extended function folds a whole string one symbol at a time, starting from the start state.

7
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Reading 0011 through the four-state machine ends in the accepting state, so 0011 is in the language.

8
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Change one arrow and the language changes. The machine is the language, written out.

9
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:12.000
A nondeterministic machine may have zero, one or many arrows for the same state and the same symbol.

10
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Run it and you do not have a state; you have a set of states that are all still possible.

11
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:28.000
An epsilon arrow reads nothing and costs nothing. The epsilon-closure adds every state reachable by epsilon alone.

12
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.000
The machine accepts if any one live state is accepting. One lucky guess is enough.

13
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:44.000
The subset construction makes one DFA state out of each reachable set of NFA states.

14
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:52.000
This NFA has four states, so sixteen subsets exist, but only four are reachable.

15
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:00.000
So an n-state NFA becomes a DFA of at most 2 to the n states. That is an upper bound.

16
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.000
The bound is an upper limit, not a floor: a four-state NFA can have a one-state minimal DFA.

17
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Two strings are distinguishable if some suffix puts one in the language and the other out.

18
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:24.000
A DFA state is a class of strings no suffix can separate, so the count is forced, not chosen.

19
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:32.000
"Ends with 10" needs three states: nothing yet, a 1 seen, a 10 seen.

20
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:40.000
"Does not contain 111" needs four: run length 0, 1, 2, and one dead state.

21
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Write the last two symbols into the state and you get seven: empty, a, b, aa, ab, ba, bb.

22
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Mark pairs that differ on acceptance, then propagate: if a pair leads to a marked pair, mark it.

23
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Three states collapse into one and two into another, so seven becomes four.

24
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:12.000
GATE 2017 Set 1 asked exactly this: the smallest machine for (a+b)*b(a+b) has four states.

25
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:20.000
To count the symbol a modulo 2 you need two states; modulo 3 you need three.

26
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:28.000
To do both at once you need the pairs, so the size is the least common multiple, which is six.

27
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Divisible by 2 but not by 3 is the same six states with only two of them accepting.

28
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:44.000
A product modulo 7 also needs six, because the six nonzero residues form a closed group.

29
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:52.000
A regular expression is built from three operations: union, concatenation and star.

30
00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Union branches, concatenation joins, star loops back. Every operation is a picture.

31
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Thompson gives each operation its own two-state wrapper and joins the fragments with epsilon arrows.

32
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:16.000
For (a|b)*abb the count is 2+2+2+2+6, which is 14 states, and the machine accepts exactly the strings ending abb.

33
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:24.000
State elimination deletes one state at a time and rewrites the arcs that survive.

34
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Delete the odd state of a two-state machine and the answer is (0 + 10*1)*.

35
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Union and intersection come from running the two machines side by side and reading both answers.

36
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Complement flips the accepting marks, so a k-state minimal machine gives a complement of at most k states.

37
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:56.000
With p states, the p+1 prefixes of a long string must share a state. That is a pigeonhole.

38
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:04.000
Repeating y keeps the string in the language, because the machine returns to the same state.

39
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:12.000
For a to the n b to the n, repeating y breaks the balance, so the language is not regular.

40
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:20.000
The recipe: assume a pumping length, choose a string, split it three ways, and name the contradiction.

